        }
      ],
      "handles": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "input",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 270,
          "portId": "root"
        }
      ],
      "inputs": [
        {
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "type": "any"
        }
      ],
      "outputs": [],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Recursive Workspace Instantiation\n\n> **Experimental plan:** this graph records an implementation attempt to inspect in the running workspace. It is not yet the final law of the model.\n\n## Governing rule\n\n**A border handle exposes a typed capability. Dragging it performs the operation defined by that capability while preserving the distinction between identity, representation, and relationship.**\n\nThe abstraction repeats at two scales:\n\n```text\nWorkspace container\n└── Graph presented as a macro-node\n    └── Graph container\n        └── Node\n```\n\nA graph contains nodes. A workspace contains graph instances backed by graph documents. Ports expose capabilities at either scale, and handles make those capabilities draggable.\n\n## One instantiate operation\n\nInstantiation is scale-independent. The receiving container determines the concrete primitive:\n\n| Drop target | Receiving scope | Result |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Blank space inside a graph | Graph container | Create a graph-owned node |\n| Blank workspace space | Workspace container | Create a graph instance backed by a graph document and declaration |\n| Compatible existing endpoint | Relationship layer at that scope | Perform the declared connection or relationship operation |\n| Interior of another open graph | That graph container | Create a node owned by that graph |\n\nBlank workspace space is not null. It is an active container surface, just as blank graph space is an active graph container surface.\n\n## Deepest accepting container\n\nThe drop resolver chooses the deepest compatible target beneath the pointer:\n\n1. A compatible node or graph endpoint.\n2. An accepting graph interior.\n3. The workspace surface.\n\nAn endpoint drop performs relationship behavior. A graph-interior drop instantiates a node. A workspace-surface drop instantiates a graph and its declaration. The preview must name the exact result before mutation.\n\n## Capability dimensions\n\nThe capability carries independent policies for:\n\n- **Identity:** new local identity, explicitly shared semantic identity, or another declared identity policy.\n- **Representation:** selected view, surface, content, glyph, or class defaults.\n- **Relationship:** none, local edge, portal relationship, or another explicit endpoint operation.\n- **Authority:** what the source offers and what the receiving container may perform.\n- **Provenance:** source graph, workspace instance, node, port, surface, and capability version.\n\nContainer scale never silently chooses semantic identity. A node created inside a graph normally gets a new graph-local ID. A graph created on the workspace gets a graph document and declaration, but whether that declaration represents a new or existing semantic identity comes from the capability.\n\n## Border projection\n\nThe port is the primitive projected onto a graph border. An output capability becomes a draggable source handle. An input capability becomes a target socket. A bidirectional port may provide both.\n\nViews, declarations, node classes, portals, content, glyphs, and other semantic nodes reach the border only through a declared public port. Edges never appear as independent draggable exports. Bridges may create incoming adjacency apertures, but they do not become creation capabilities unless a port explicitly grants that behavior.\n\nBorder handles remain stable while graph content semantic-zooms. Incoming adjacency occupies the top or left; outgoing or creatable capabilities occupy the right or bottom. The workspace minimap shows graph frames and adjacency, while graph-level navigation can still describe a large focused graph.\n\n## Transactions\n\nA graph-interior drop creates a node owned by the receiving graph, with a new graph-local ID unless identity policy says otherwise. A workspace drop creates a graph document, declaration, root port, workspace instance, placement, and focus. A compatible endpoint drop executes the declared relationship operation.\n\nEach result is one undoable transaction. Source graphs remain unchanged. Only the persistent receiving document becomes dirty. Workspace arrangement remains workspace state.\n\n## Remaining questions\n\n- What is the minimum authority vocabulary for offering and accepting capabilities at both scopes?\n- Which identity and provenance tuple survives repeated drops and multiple workspace instances?\n- Which concrete capability schemas should replace or extend the current `drag-create` plus `create-node` behavior without breaking existing graphs?\n\nThe first implementation should make the container resolver and capability interpreter explicit, preserve the existing local-edge model, and let us inspect the interaction before promoting this experiment into a durable contract."
        },
        "interfaceContract": {
          "version": 1,
          "receivesViewContent": true
        },
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "workspace-graph-node-projection-declaration"
        }
      }
    },
